<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mazyar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/840">840</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Mazyar ibn Qarin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mazyar was the last prince of the Qarinvand dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a>, in the mountains south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>. He converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a> and governed Tabaristan, but in 839 he rebelled against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutasim">al-Mu'tasim</a> and the Tahirid governors of Khurasan. Betrayed and captured, he was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a> and executed in 840. Later Iranian tradition remembered him as a defender of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> against Arab rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mazyar.jpg" alt="Mazyar" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mohamadhosein.a · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mazyar_of_the_Qarinvand_dynasty.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mazyar belonged to the Qarinvand dynasty, descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukhra">Sukhra</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-karen">House of Karen</a>, de facto ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> from 484 to 493; Sukhra's son Karin received land south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amol">Amol</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-i">Khosrow I</a>. Mazyar succeeded his father Qarin ibn Vindadhhurmuzd around 817, but the Bavandid ruler Shahriyar I drove him out, and his cousin Vinda-Umid handed him over to Shahriyar. He escaped to the court of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a>, where he met the astrologer Yahya ibn al-Munajjim, embraced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and took the name Abu'l-Hasan Muhammad.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">Al-Ma'mun</a> gave him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruyan">Ruyan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damavand">Damavand</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> and made him co-governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> with Musa ibn Hafs. Returning with Abbasid troops in 822/3, he exiled his brother Quhyar and his nephew Qarin I, and in 825/6 he invaded the Bavandid lands and captured Shahriyar's son Shapur, whom he later had beheaded. With Vinda-Umid killed, he united the highlands and took the titles of the Dabuyid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farrukhan-the-great">Farrukhan the Great</a>: Gil-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a>, Ispahbadh and Padishkhwargarshah. He built mosques, but the Muslims of Tabaristan regarded his rule as oppressive.</p><p>When the Tahirid Abdallah ibn Tahir demanded the land tax and the release of Muhammad ibn Musa, whom Mazyar had imprisoned, he refused, and Abdallah accused him before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutasim">al-Mu'tasim</a> of infidelity and tyranny. Mazyar rebelled, with wide support among the native <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrians</a>. Five armies entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a>; Qarin I went over to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>, the people of Sari revolted, and Quhyar captured his brother and handed him over.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He was executed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a>, and his body was gibbeted beside that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babak-khorramdin">Babak Khorramdin</a>. Quhyar was soon killed by his own Daylamite soldiers, ending the dynasty, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahirid-dynasty">Tahirids</a> ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> with Qarin I as their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. In the Tarikh-e-Tabaristan of the medieval historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-isfandiyar">Ibn Isfandiyar</a>, Mazyar claims an oath with Afshin and Babak to take the country back from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, and twentieth-century Iranian nationalist historiography counts him among the national heroes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mazyar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Mazyar ibn Qarin</b> converted to Islam and served al-Ma'mun, but rebelled against the caliph al-Mu'tasim and the Tahirid dynasty in 839.</blockquote><a href="/state/qarinvand-dynasty">Qarinvand dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36,51&amp;year=830" aria-label="Tabaristan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Mazyar"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4380 4463h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4380 4463h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36,51&amp;year=830">What was here in 830 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
